n  is acknowledged so yeah i do feel that  in these cases  a simple disclaimer can really go a long  way you know thin privilege is something  that you know i acknowledge and talk  about  regularly on this channel so much so  that some people have  called me out on it thinking that it's  total bs it's not bs  but whiteness is another privilege that  i do believe i need to acknowledge more  often  that's definitely something that i'm  personally working on  now most importantly i want to say that  while i think it's important that we  all work towards respecting our body and  fostering positive beliefs about our  body  even communicating that if you are thin  and a white influencer  it's also important to knowledge that  it's a hell of a lot easier for someone  like me to find  body positivity without a movement  behind me to support me  when my body is praised by society by  default  the marginalized bodies for whom the  body positivity movement was originally  intended for  do not have that same privilege so for  those of us who  do live in privileged socially desirable  bodies that is thin white and able we  maybe need to  take a step back from the body  positivity movement  to allow its original intended  benefactors to shine we can  and we should still be allies and still  support the tenets of body positivity  when it comes to our use of the movement  for our personal gain  let's say for marketing it perhaps needs  to come with a different name  a colleague of mine rebecca scritchfield  uses body kindness for example  other ideas are body neutrality our body  respect body love body freedom body  confidence  these might better articulate what we  stand for without encroaching on  someone else's really important  territory okay so moving on and again  a reminder that this video is not a  response to the article  um i actually had other messages in the  original video that i want to articulate  so i do want to repeat them again here  because  they help to express my professional  views  and how they've ultimately evolved over  time i do not believe that  all intentional weight loss efforts or  changes in diet  with a desire to lose weight are  examples of disordered eating  poor body image hating your body or  mental instability i  do not think that diets intrinsically  lead to eating disorders there are  subsets of the population for whom  engaging in any kind of purposeful  restriction is going to be problematic  and unhealthy  physically and or psychologically but  there are also a lot of people  who can count calories or get on the  scale every week or cut out certain food  groups or use a dieting app  without it impacting their mental health  they can do these things for periods of  time  and then you know let them go when they  aren't working for them rather than  obsessed the point  of it taking over their life so if you  find that  some of these things work for you and  this is what improves your physical and  emotional health and  sense of happiness then you should feel  empowered  not ashamed to do these things now  second  being fat does not mean you're unhealthy  but we do have evidence to suggest that  excess weight can play a role in some  health issues blood pressure blood  sugars  joint pain infertility heart disease  cancer risk kidney and liver disease  sleep disorders etc like i said before  to deny this would be an insult to  people with this lived experience and  also to the research  but weight is just one variable or risk  factor for  any disease and thin people suffer from  these diseases  all the time as well however thin people  are often offered  alternative treatments that fat people  are not  because the medical system tends to see  a large body and just sends that patient  away to change it so a lot of  haze proponents will say that the data  linking weight to disease is due to  weight stigma in the health care system  and therefore poor medical care i would  say yes  that is absolutely happening and it  probably does  play a devastating role but i don't  think that the excess weight itself  is completely benign across the board so  i think unfortunately we see a potential  health problem  aka being overweight turn into a larger  health problem  when equitable and fair health care  isn't accessed  third i've said this before in my video  here but i believe in meeting people  where they are  even though i know that most people who  try to lose weight will  gain it back statistically if this is  what an individual wants to do  i fully respect that and i can get on  board i fully  appreciate why anyone would want to lose  weight especially  because living in a larger body in an  aesthetically driven society  is surely really uncomfortable so i  think it's important that as a nutrition  professional who ultimately  wants to help people that i hold space  for those conversations  rather than just shut them down and push  them out and while my approach will  always be to focus on  health promoting behaviors while letting  the weight kind of do what it will  i think it's still important to work  with a client  and to work with their goals and finally  to reiterate and of course conclude  as i have no lived experience in a  marginalized body the body positivity  movement  is not for me to use for personal gain  and again while i haven't really made  body positive be part of my personal  marketing i agree that  some of us maybe should refrain from  monetizing from it by calling ourselves  body positive dietitians body freedom  dietitians body confidence dietitians  great but maybe we just have to mute our  use of that hashtag  to let those who it was originally  intended for be heard  so i will make space for those bodies  that need to be uplifted and will  support from the sidelines again i  acknowledge we all have body struggles  regardless of size shape or race  and all of those struggles are valid but  we create our own movement to explore  these  while leaving the body positive movement  to those who need it most whether that  be  those who are fat black lgbtq disabled  or other  marginalized populations so friends i  think that's where i'm at and i'm not  done  i'm still learning and my opinions and  beliefs will probably change again it  would be a lot easier for me  to not address these issues or to like  pull the wool over my eyes and pretend  this is not an issue but it's obviously  weighing on me heavily and i think it's  a really important discussion to have i  mean the youtube wellness space  shouldn't just be about vegan smoothie  bowls and  keto fat bombs we need to be talking  real issues  too and on that note that is all that i  have for you guys today  i really really really want to hear what  you have to say and also please be  kind in the comments this one was  admittedly  a really hard one for me to articulate  because i often feel  really vulnerable being in the middle of  what i often see  as opposing ideologies you know the haze  folks and the weight loss folks but if  you like this video please be sure to  give it  the thumbs up and also check out the  links to some of the folks i interviewed  today below  and i'm also going to be sharing a list  of larger bodied or bipod creators below  so that you can check that out  in your own time also subscribe to the  channel and i will see you next time on  abby's kitchen  bye
 

